@meta
id: 20-05
title: Read the register list
chapter: 20
type: lesson
skills: register-named
par: 32
minutes: 5
vscode: open the clipboard history panel and squint
vimrc: let @a="    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,\n" | let @b="    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,\n" | let @c="    @inject(PaymentService) private paymentService: PaymentService\n"
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Registers are invisible until you ask. `:reg` lists them all; `:reg ab`
lists just `a` and `b`. Each row shows the register's name, its type —
`l` for whole lines, `c` for part of a line — and its contents, with
`^J` standing in for a line break.

For this lesson the dojo has loaded three registers before you arrive,
as if you had yanked three candidate lines earlier in the session and
lost track of which is which. One of them is the constructor line that
injects `PaymentService` into `OrderProvider`, in the codebase's style:
`protected`, with a trailing comma. The other two are close but wrong.

Look at the list, decide, and put the right one directly under the
`orderRepository` injection.

@keys
| `:reg` | every register and its contents |
| `:reg abc` | just those three |
| `"bp` | put from `b` |

@hints
`:reg abc` Enter shows the three candidates side by side. Press Enter
again to dismiss the list. Only one of them is `protected` *and* ends
with a comma.
--
`/protected` Enter lands on the `orderRepository` injection: it is the
only `protected` in the file. (`/orderRepos` would stop at the import
first, because `ignorecase` makes it match `OrderRepository` too.)
--
`:reg abc` Enter Enter, `/protected` Enter, `"bp`.

@solution
    :reg abc<Enter><Enter> /protected<Enter> "bp :wq<Enter>

Reading the list is the skill, and the codes in the type column are
worth a moment: `l` means the register was filled by a linewise
operation (`yy`, `dd`) and `p` will put it on a line of its own; `c`
means characterwise (`yiw`, `dw`) and `p` will splice it into the
current line. When a put lands in the wrong shape, this column is why.

`:reg` with no argument shows everything, including registers you have
not met yet: `"0` through `"9`, `"-`, `"%`, `":`, `"/`, and a few more.
The rest of this chapter goes through them. If you ever see a `"q`
full of keystrokes, that is a macro, and chapter 21 is where you find
out how it got there.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}
